@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "UPDATE Operation";
}

<section>

    <!-- PAGE INTRODUCTION -->

    <div class="mb-5">

        <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            UPDATE Operation
        </h1>

        <p class="lead text-muted">
            The UPDATE statement is used to modify existing records
            in a SQL Server table.
        </p>

        <p>
            When information already stored in a table needs to be
            changed, we use the UPDATE statement. For example, we can
            update a student's city, age, fees or any other existing
            information.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- WHAT IS UPDATE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            What is UPDATE?
        </h2>

        <p>
            The <strong>UPDATE</strong> statement is used to change
            existing values in one or more rows of a table.
        </p>

        <p>
            The SET clause specifies the new value, while the WHERE
            clause identifies which records should be modified.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- SYNTAX -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            UPDATE Syntax
        </h2>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE table_name
SET column_name = new_value
WHERE condition;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-warning border-0 mt-4">

            <strong>Important:</strong>
            Always use a suitable WHERE condition when you want to
            update specific records. Without WHERE, all rows can be
            updated.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SAMPLE TABLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Example Table
        </h2>

        <p>
            We will use the following Students table for the examples.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                        <td>Nashik</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>104</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- UPDATE SINGLE RECORD -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Update a Single Record
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose we want to change Rahul's city from Pune to Mumbai.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE Students
SET City = 'Mumbai'
WHERE StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- OUTPUT -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Output
        </h2>

        <p>
            After executing the query, Rahul's City value is changed
            from Pune to Mumbai.
        </p>

        <div class="table-responsive">

            <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                <thead class="table-dark">

                    <tr>
                        <th>StudentId</th>
                        <th>StudentName</th>
                        <th>Age</th>
                        <th>City</th>
                    </tr>

                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>101</td>
                        <td>Rahul</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>102</td>
                        <td>Priya</td>
                        <td>23</td>
                        <td>Mumbai</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>103</td>
                        <td>Amit</td>
                        <td>21</td>
                        <td>Nashik</td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>104</td>
                        <td>Neha</td>
                        <td>22</td>
                        <td>Pune</td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- UPDATE MULTIPLE COLUMNS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Update Multiple Columns
        </h2>

        <p>
            Multiple columns can be updated in the same UPDATE
            statement.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE Students
SET Age = 24,
    City = 'Pune'
WHERE StudentId = 102;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This query changes both the Age and City of the student
            whose StudentId is 102.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- UPDATE USING CONDITION -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            UPDATE Using a Condition
        </h2>

        <p>
            We can use conditions to update records that satisfy a
            particular requirement.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE Students
SET City = 'Pune'
WHERE Age = 22;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            This statement updates the City for all students whose
            Age is 22.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- UPDATE WITHOUT WHERE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            UPDATE Without WHERE
        </h2>

        <p>
            If the WHERE clause is omitted, the UPDATE statement
            affects all rows in the table.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE Students
SET City = 'Pune';</code></pre>

        </div>

        <div class="alert alert-danger border-0 mt-4">

            <strong>Warning:</strong>
            The above statement updates the City column for every
            record in the Students table.

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- UPDATE STRING VALUE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Updating a String Value
        </h2>

        <p>
            String values are written inside single quotation marks.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE Students
SET StudentName = 'Rahul Sharma'
WHERE StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- UPDATE NUMERIC VALUE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Updating a Numeric Value
        </h2>

        <p>
            Numeric values do not require quotation marks.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE Students
SET Age = 25
WHERE StudentId = 101;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- PRACTICAL EXAMPLE -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Practical Example
        </h2>

        <p>
            Suppose a student changes their city after admission.
            We can update the student's existing record using
            StudentId.
        </p>

        <div class="code-block">

            <div class="code-lang-tag">
                SQL
            </div>

            <pre class="mb-0"><code>UPDATE Students
SET City = 'Bangalore'
WHERE StudentId = 103;</code></pre>

        </div>

        <p class="mt-3">
            Only the record with StudentId 103 will be modified.
        </p>

    </section>


    <!-- IMPORTANT POINTS -->

    <section class="mb-5">

        <div class="alert alert-info border-0">

            <h4 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Important Points
            </h4>

            <ul class="mb-0">

                <li class="mb-2">
                    UPDATE is used to modify existing records.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    SET is used to specify the new value.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    WHERE is used to identify the records to update.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Multiple columns can be updated in one statement.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Without WHERE, all rows may be updated.
                </li>

                <li>
                    String values should be enclosed in single quotes.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </section>


    <!-- SUMMARY -->

    <section class="mb-4">

        <div class="alert alert-primary border-0">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Summary
            </h2>

            <p class="mb-0">
                In this section, we learned how the UPDATE statement
                modifies existing records in a SQL Server table. We
                covered updating a single column, multiple columns,
                using conditions, and the importance of using the
                WHERE clause.
            </p>

        </div>

    </section>


